If you need to build a command-line tool with flag parsing and can't decide on the right ecosystem, give Ruby a look.
You don't even need to pull in third-party gems to get started - [OptionParser](https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/OptionParser.html) is built right into the standard library. All it takes is defining your help message and declaring your flags with its expressive block syntax.
Take a look at the snippet above - it's remarkable how clean and readable CLI flag declarations look using [OptionParser's DSL](https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/optparse/tutorial_rdoc.html).

How do you usually build CLI tools? Which languages and libraries do you reach for in your projects?

```ruby
require 'optparse'

module Arithmetic
  class CLI
    OPS = { 'add' => :+, 'sub' => :-, 'mul' => :*, 'div' => :/ }.freeze
    def initialize(args)
      @args = args.dup
      @options = {}
    end
    def run
      parser = OptionParser.new do |opts|
        opts.banner = 'Usage: ruby arithmetic.rb -n <numbers> -o <operation>'
        opts.separator ''
        opts.separator 'Example:'
        opts.separator '  ruby arithmetic.rb -n 1,2,3 -o add'
        opts.separator '  1+2+3=6'
        opts.separator ''
        opts.on(
          '-n', '--numbers <1,2,3,...>', Array,
          'Comma-separated list of integers'
        ) do |nums|
          raise OptionParser::InvalidArgument,
            'must not be empty' if nums.empty?
          @options[:nums] = nums.map(&:to_i)
        end
        opts.on(
          '-o', '--operation <add|sub|mul|div>', String,
          "Operation: #{OPS.keys.join(', ')}"
        ) do |op|
          raise OptionParser::InvalidArgument,
            "must be one of: #{OPS.keys.join(', ')}" unless OPS.key?(op)
          @options[:op] = op
        end
      end
      parser.parse!(@args)
      return puts(parser.help) unless @options[:nums] && @options[:op]
      op = OPS[@options[:op]]
      nums = @options[:nums]
      puts "#{nums.join(op.to_s)}=#{nums.reduce(op)}"
    end
  end
end
Arithmetic::CLI.new(ARGV).run

# Save to arithmetic.rb and run:
# $ ruby arithmetic.rb -h
# $ ruby arithmetic.rb -n 7,8,9 -o add
```
